      },
      "worked": "Let the cyclic quadrilateral be $PQRS$, with consecutive sides $PQ=a$, $QR=b$, $RS=c$, $SP=d$. Draw $PR=e$. Put $\\angle PQR=\\theta$; the opposite angle is $\\angle PSR=180^\\circ-\\theta$.\n\nUsing the area rule and $\\sin(180^\\circ-\\theta)=\\sin\\theta$,\n$$\\begin{align*}A&=\\frac12ab\\sin\\theta+\\frac12cd\\sin(180^\\circ-\\theta)\\\\A&=\\frac12(ab+cd)\\sin\\theta.\\end{align*}$$\nThe cosine rule in the two triangles gives\n$$\\begin{align*}e^2&=a^2+b^2-2ab\\cos\\theta\\\\e^2&=c^2+d^2+2cd\\cos\\theta.\\end{align*}$$\nEquating these expressions and rearranging,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\cos\\theta&=\\frac{a^2+b^2-c^2-d^2}{2(ab+cd)}.\\end{align*}$$\nUse $\\sin^2\\theta=1-\\cos^2\\theta$ and $2s=a+b+c+d$:\n$$\\begin{align*}16A^2&=4(ab+cd)^2\\sin^2\\theta\\\\16A^2&=4(ab+cd)^2-(a^2+b^2-c^2-d^2)^2\\\\16A^2&=\\bigl((c+d)^2-(a-b)^2\\bigr)\\bigl((a+b)^2-(c-d)^2\\bigr)\\\\16A^2&=(2s-2a)(2s-2b)(2s-2c)(2s-2d)\\\\16A^2&=16(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)(s-d).\\end{align*}$$\nThe difference-of-squares factors in the third row are the four factors in the fourth row. Hence,\n$$\\begin{align*}A^2&=(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)(s-d)\\\\A&=\\sqrt{(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)(s-d)}.\\end{align*}$$\nTake the positive square root because $A$ is an area.",
